explanation:
This complete example receives a new answer before an old one.
```javascript
let latestId = 0;
const cancelledIds = new Set();
let visibleResult = "";
function beginSearch() {
  if (latestId > 0) {
    cancelledIds.add(latestId);
  }
  latestId++;
  return latestId;
}
function receiveResult(id, text) {
  if (id === latestId && !cancelledIds.has(id)) {
    visibleResult = text;
  }
}
const first = beginSearch();
const second = beginSearch();
receiveResult(second, "mountain answer");
receiveResult(first, "river answer");
console.log(visibleResult);
```
It prints `mountain answer`. Beginning the second request cancels the first id in the set. Receiving the first response later fails the current-id test.

This second standalone fixture tests an old response arriving first.
```javascript
let latestId = 0;
let visibleResult = "";
function beginSearch() {
  latestId++;
  return latestId;
}
function receiveResult(id, text) {
  if (id === latestId) {
    visibleResult = text;
  }
}
const first = beginSearch();
const second = beginSearch();
receiveResult(first, "outdated");
console.log(visibleResult === "");
receiveResult(second, "current");
console.log(visibleResult);
```
It displays true, then current. Even before the newer answer arrives, the earlier request is already outdated. The id check alone protects which result may be displayed.

