explanation:
This complete valid-input example includes empty input.
```javascript
function total(values) {
  if (!Array.isArray(values) || values.some(v =>
    typeof v !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(v) || v < 0
  )) {
    throw new TypeError("Expected finite nonnegative numbers in an array");
  }
  return values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0);
}
console.log(total([4, 6, 0]));
console.log(total([]));
```
It prints 10 and 0. The guard rejects bad inputs before reduce runs. The initial reducer value zero defines the empty total. Some returns false for an empty array, so empty input passes validation.

Here is an independently runnable malformed-input example.
```javascript
function total(values) {
  if (!Array.isArray(values) || values.some(v =>
    typeof v !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(v) || v < 0
  )) {
    throw new TypeError("Expected finite nonnegative numbers in an array");
  }
  return values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0);
}
try {
  console.log(total(["4"]));
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error instanceof TypeError);
  console.log(error.message);
}
```
It prints true and the expected-input message. The quoted 4 was rejected, so no numerical total was displayed. `instanceof TypeError` checks the error's kind.

